# Rules: Signals & Right of Way
# answer-mode: choice
# choice-count: 4
# Based on ICBC's Learn to Drive Smart guide. Hand-written — verify against
# the current guide before trusting an answer.

In BC, a flashing green light at an intersection means:
---
a pedestrian-controlled signal — proceed with caution
~ you have the right of way over all traffic
~ the light is about to turn yellow
~ a transit-priority signal for buses

A flashing red light at an intersection means:
---
stop, then proceed when it is safe
~ stop and wait for the light to turn green
~ slow down and proceed with caution
~ the signal is broken — treat it as a yield

A flashing yellow light at an intersection means:
---
slow down and proceed with caution
~ stop, then proceed when it is safe
~ speed up to clear the intersection
~ pedestrians are crossing — stop

A steady yellow light means:
---
stop, unless you cannot stop safely
~ clear the intersection as fast as possible
~ proceed — it has the same meaning as green
~ stop and wait, exactly like a red light

A green arrow shown with a red light means:
---
you may go in the direction of the arrow, yielding to traffic and pedestrians in the intersection
~ wait for the full green before turning
~ only transit vehicles may turn
~ the arrow is advisory and the red light governs

Two vehicles arrive at a four-way stop at the same time. Who goes first?
---
the vehicle on the right
~ the vehicle on the left
~ the vehicle going straight, regardless of position
~ whichever vehicle moves first

When may you turn left at a red light in BC?
---
from a one-way street onto a one-way street, after stopping, when clear
~ never — left turns on red are illegal in BC
~ from any street onto a one-way street, without stopping
~ whenever there is no oncoming traffic

A school bus has its red lights flashing. What must you do?
---
stop, whether you are behind it or approaching from the front
~ stop only if you are behind the bus
~ slow to 30 km/h and pass with caution
~ pass only on the left after honking

An emergency vehicle approaches with lights and siren on. You must:
---
pull over to the right and stop
~ speed up and clear the area
~ stop exactly where you are, even mid-intersection
~ pull over only if it is in your lane

A driver's left arm pointing straight out the window signals:
---
a left turn
~ a right turn
~ slowing down or stopping
~ overtaking on the left

A driver's left arm bent upward signals:
---
a right turn
~ a left turn
~ slowing down or stopping
~ a lane change to the left

A driver's left arm pointing down signals:
---
slowing down or stopping
~ a right turn
~ a left turn
~ reversing

A broken yellow line on your side of the centre line means:
---
you may pass when it is safe
~ passing is never allowed
~ the lane is reserved for buses
~ two-way left turns are allowed

A double solid yellow centre line means:
---
passing is not allowed in either direction
~ passing is allowed with extra caution
~ only the side with more lanes may pass
~ the road becomes one-way ahead

What does the two-second rule measure?
---
your following distance behind the vehicle ahead
~ how long to wait at a stop sign
~ your reaction time to a hazard
~ the gap needed to merge onto a highway

Yellow diamond-shaped signs are:
---
warning signs
~ regulatory signs
~ construction signs
~ information signs

Orange signs mark:
---
construction and maintenance zones
~ school zones
~ wildlife corridors
~ snow routes
